#### First Citation by Anshul Adve <https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17935> on Sept 22, 2025

The uniqueness results in Anshul's paper rely heavily on the techniques covered in my paper.  Very well done!

The whole genesis of Inverse Problems in Spectral Theory comes from Mark Kac's "Can you hear the shape of a drum?"

My paper's introduction describes the state of the art as of 1997. It is clear that the Laplace spectrum alone is inadequate.  However, supplementing it with **structure constants** &mdash; here the triple product integrals of an eigenbasis &mdash; holds a lot of promise in terms of composing the map
```math
(M,g,\set{e^i}) \mapsto \set{\lambda_i, M^{i,j,k}}.
```
We have to understand the image of that map before we can describe its Inverse.

Anshul's present work on unit tangent bundles of compact hyperbolic 2-orbifolds accomplishes both, and lights the initial path forward.

